Code statements:
```c
spin_lock(&vnode->lock);
_debug("break callback");
if (list_empty(&vnode->granted_locks) &&
    !list_empty(&vnode->pending_locks))
    afs_lock_may_be_available(vnode);
spin_unlock(&vnode->lock);
```

Code statements:
```c
spin_lock(&vnode->lock);
_debug("break callback");
if (list_empty(&vnode->granted_locks) &&
    !list_empty(&vnode->pending_locks))
    afs_lock_may_be_available(vnode);
spin_unlock(&vnode->lock);
```

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
spin_lock(&vnode->lock);
afs_lock_may_be_available(vnode);
```
Reason:
The `__afs_break_callback()` function holds `vnode->lock` while calling `afs_lock_may_be_available()`, which also attempts to acquire the same lock. This creates a potential deadlock situation due to double locking on the same resource.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
// spin_lock(&vnode->lock); // Removed
if (vnode->lock_state == AFS_VNODE_LOCK_WAITING_FOR_CB)
    afs_lock_may_be_available(vnode);
```
Reason:
The patch removes the lock acquisition in `__afs_break_callback()`, preventing the double locking issue. It also simplifies the logic by directly checking the `lock_state`, eliminating unnecessary checks on the lock queues.

